# PostgreSQL's aset.c under Sublet.
#
# Applied on top of aset-capstone.patch, which is the change any machine with
# sixteen-byte pointers needs.  This one is the discipline: every chunk gets
# bounds of its own, every free revokes what the program still holds, and a
# context's whole heap dies in one revocation.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, WHICH IS THE NUMBER A7 REPORTS
#
#   added     149 lines of code, 268 of comment
#   removed   245 lines of code, 123 of comment
#
# So the allocator gets SMALLER by 96 lines of code.  That is not a trick of
# counting and it is worth saying out loud: what disappears is the teardown.
# AllocSetReset and AllocSetDelete each walked the block list and freed the
# blocks one at a time, and AllocSetCheck walked every chunk of every block.
# One revocation replaces the first two walks entirely, and the third cannot
# be done at all, for a reason given at the function.
#
# WHAT CHANGES, AND WHY EACH ONE IS FORCED
#
# Every change below is one of three facts about capabilities, and none of
# them is a choice.
#
#   A capability cannot reach out of the object it was given for.  So a chunk
#   cannot name its block by the distance back to it, the keeper block cannot
#   be the context header's address plus a constant, and the manager cannot
#   form a pointer to a chunk it was not handed.  Each becomes a field or an
#   index: KeeperBlock, ChunkGetBlock, the chunk walker in AllocSetCheck.
#
#   A revocation cannot spare part of what it covers.  So the context header
#   leaves the sub-pool, into a table of its own, and the keeper block is
#   revoked with everything else and carved again at the same size.
#
#   A revoked chunk cannot be read or written.  So the free list of a size
#   class leaves the freed chunk and becomes an index into a side table, the
#   chunk's header is written when it is handed out rather than when it is
#   freed, and AllocSetStats walks a free list without touching a chunk.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, WHICH IS A7's CONDITION
#
# The policy.  The same size classes, the same block sizes, the same doubling,
# the same keeper, the same order of chunks within a block, the same free
# lists per class, the same context freelist.  ALLOC_BLOCKHDRSZ stays at
# eighty bytes even though no header occupies them any more, because that
# number decides allocChunkLimit and therefore the whole ladder, and the arm
# this is measured against is the same manager without the discipline.
# Eighty bytes a block are spent to keep the two comparable.
#
# THE ONE POLICY CHANGE, REPORTED AS ONE
#
# In-place realloc of a chunk above allocChunkLimit becomes a copy, because a
# region can be split and not joined and no primitive grows one.  The
# recording prices it: of 1 228 019 allocations on the pgbench tpcb rung there
# are two reallocs and one takes that path, the timezone parser at startup
# with 5 120 bytes.  See experiments/a11/postgres/reallocs.py in the paper.
#
